course: dictionary
lesson: 836
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and benefit is one of them. The sentence tells you which meaning fits. «Many bees have become fewer, which matters because about three quarters of the main food crops benefit from pollinators.» Here, to benefit from something means to be helped by it.
explain: Benefit can also mean something good or helpful that you get from something, like «the benefits of exercise, sleep and healthy food». Look at the words around it to decide which meaning is right.
explain: Benefits can also mean money. «When people lose their job, the government can give some money, called benefits, to help pay for basics.» The job and the help for basics are the clues: the money is paid to people who need help.
explain: To check your answer, put your meaning in place of the word. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A benefit is something good or helpful that you get from something.
fact: To benefit from something means to be helped by it.
fact: Benefits can mean money the government gives to people who need help, for example when they have no job or are ill. This is used in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: A benefit can also be an event held to raise money for a cause, like a benefit concert.
fact: «For the benefit of» means to help someone, and «give someone the benefit of the doubt» means to believe them when you are not sure.
exercise: «About three quarters of the main food crops benefit from pollinators.» What does benefit mean here? | Are hidden from the sun | Are paid money by the government | Are held at a party to raise money | *Are helped by pollinators
exercise: «When people lose their job, the government can give some money, called benefits.» What are benefits here? | Good things you get from exercise | Holes where bees live | *Money the government gives to people who need help | A concert held to raise money
exercise: Which sentence uses benefit to mean an event? | *Our school held a benefit concert to raise money for the library. | Regular sleep has many benefits for your health. | Sam will benefit from practising his spellings every day. | Please give him the benefit of the doubt.
test: «Before an operation, doctors explain its risks and benefits.» What does benefits mean here? | Money paid to the patient | Rules that the nurses must follow | A party for the hospital | *Good things that come from the operation
test: «The race was run for the benefit of the local hospital.» What does for the benefit of mean here? | To stop the hospital from opening | To hold a race for fun only | *To help the hospital | To give the hospital a new name
exam: Maya says, «I'll give him the benefit of the doubt.» What does that mean? | To forget everything he said | To give him some money | To give him a prize for his work | *To believe him when you are not sure
